    content: `
      <h2>Overview of the US F-1 Student Visa</h2>
      <p>The F-1 visa is the most common visa type for international students enrolled in academic programs (universities, colleges, language training) in the United States. It allows you to study full-time and, in some cases, work on campus or through Optional Practical Training (OPT).</p>
      <h2>Step 1: Receive Your I-20 Form</h2>
      <p>Before you can apply for an F-1 visa, you must be accepted by a SEVP-certified institution (Student and Exchange Visitor Program). Once accepted, the school's Designated School Official (DSO) will issue you a Form I-20 — your most important document throughout the visa process.</p>
      <h2>Step 2: Pay the SEVIS Fee</h2>
      <p>The Student and Exchange Visitor Information System (SEVIS) fee is $350 for F-1 applicants. Pay it at fmjfee.com. Keep the payment confirmation — you'll need it at your embassy interview. This fee is non-refundable.</p>
      <h2>Step 3: Complete the DS-160 Online Application</h2>
      <p>The DS-160 is the online nonimmigrant visa application form. Fill it out carefully at ceac.state.gov. You'll need your passport, I-20, photo, travel history, and SEVIS ID. Print the confirmation barcode page once submitted.</p>
      <h2>Step 4: Schedule Your Visa Interview</h2>
      <p>Visit the US Embassy website in your country to schedule an appointment. During peak season (June–August), wait times can stretch to 4–12 weeks in some countries. Apply for your visa at least 120 days before your program start date, but no earlier than 365 days before.</p>
      <h2>Step 5: Prepare Your Documents</h2>
      <p>Bring to the interview: valid passport (at least 6 months beyond your stay), DS-160 confirmation page, visa fee payment receipt, SEVIS fee receipt, I-20 form, university acceptance letter, financial evidence (bank statements, scholarship letters), academic transcripts, standardised test scores (TOEFL/SAT/GRE), and evidence of ties to your home country.</p>
      <h2>Step 6: Attend the Embassy Interview</h2>
      <p>The visa officer will ask about your field of study, future plans, funding sources, and ties to your home country. Answer clearly and confidently. The most common F-1 rejection reason is the officer not being convinced you will return home after graduation — so emphasise your home-country connections, family, and career plans there.</p>
      <h2>Step 7: Receive Your Visa and Prepare to Travel</h2>
      <p>If approved, your passport will be returned with the visa stamped inside. You may enter the US up to 30 days before your program start date. Upon entry, maintain your F-1 status by enrolling full-time and reporting any changes to your DSO.</p>
      <h2>Common Reasons for F-1 Visa Rejection</h2>
      <p>The most frequent rejection reasons include: inadequate financial documentation, weak academic background relative to the program, insufficient ties to home country, previous immigration violations, and inconsistent answers during the interview.</p>
    `,
