    content: `
      <h2>Why Your English Test Choice Matters</h2>
      <p>English proficiency tests are gatekeepers to some of the world's top universities and scholarship programs. The wrong choice can delay your application, cost you money, or require a retake. In 2026, the landscape has shifted considerably as Duolingo gains wider acceptance.</p>
      <h2>IELTS (International English Language Testing System)</h2>
      <p>IELTS is the world's most widely taken English language test, with over 3 million tests administered annually. It is accepted by over 11,000 institutions in 140+ countries including all UK, Australian, and Canadian universities, and most US and European institutions. It tests Listening, Reading, Writing, and Speaking. There are two formats: IELTS Academic (for university applications) and IELTS General Training (for migration/work). The Speaking test is conducted face-to-face with a trained examiner. Cost: approximately $250–$310 USD. Results valid for 2 years.</p>
      <h2>TOEFL iBT (Test of English as a Foreign Language)</h2>
      <p>The TOEFL is the preferred English test for US and Canadian universities. It is administered by ETS and accepted at over 11,500 institutions globally. It tests Reading, Listening, Speaking (into a microphone), and Writing. The test is fully computer-based, making it more structured and predictable than IELTS. It is less widely accepted in the UK and Australia. Cost: approximately $185–$300 USD. Results valid for 2 years.</p>
      <h2>Duolingo English Test (DET)</h2>
      <p>The Duolingo English Test is the newest entrant and has grown rapidly since COVID-19 forced universities to accept remote testing. It can be taken at home on a computer in just 1 hour. It costs only $65 USD — significantly cheaper than IELTS or TOEFL. As of 2026, it is accepted by over 5,000 institutions including MIT, Harvard, and Stanford, though some programs still require IELTS or TOEFL. Results are delivered within 48 hours.</p>
      <h2>Head-to-Head Comparison</h2>
      <p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> IELTS = widest (UK/Australia/Canada/US). TOEFL = strongest in USA. Duolingo = growing but not universal.</p>
      <p><strong>Cost:</strong> Duolingo ($65) beats TOEFL ($185–300) and IELTS ($250–310) decisively.</p>
      <p><strong>Convenience:</strong> Duolingo (at-home, 1 hour) is the most convenient. TOEFL (test centres, 3 hours) and IELTS (test centres, up to 2 hours 45 min + separate speaking session) require more planning.</p>
      <p><strong>Speaking component:</strong> IELTS's live interview format is preferred by UK and Australian institutions. TOEFL and Duolingo use computer-based speaking assessments.</p>
      <p><strong>Score validity:</strong> All three are valid for 2 years.</p>
      <h2>Which Should You Choose?</h2>
      <p>If applying to UK, Australia, or Canadian universities: take IELTS. If applying primarily to US universities: take TOEFL (or confirm IELTS/Duolingo acceptance first). If you're cost-constrained or applying to flexible programs: check if Duolingo is accepted and take advantage of its low cost and convenience. Many students take both IELTS and Duolingo for maximum coverage.</p>
    `,
