    content: `
      <h2>What Is the Fulbright Program?</h2>
      <p>Founded in 1946 by Senator J. William Fulbright, the Fulbright Program is the US government's flagship exchange program, managed by the Bureau of Educational and Cultural Affairs (ECA) of the US Department of State. It operates in over 160 countries and funds approximately 8,000 new grants annually.</p>
      <h2>Types of Fulbright Grants</h2>
      <p><strong>Fulbright Foreign Student Program:</strong> For international students to pursue graduate study or research in the USA. <strong>Fulbright Scholar Program:</strong> For faculty and professionals to lecture and research in the USA. <strong>Fulbright U.S. Student Program:</strong> For American citizens to study, research, or teach abroad. <strong>Fulbright-Nehru (India), Fulbright-Hays:</strong> Special bilateral programs.</p>
      <h2>Benefits</h2>
      <p>Benefits typically include full tuition, a monthly living stipend, round-trip airfare, health insurance, and a book/equipment allowance. English language training is available for eligible applicants. The exact benefit package varies by country and program type.</p>
      <h2>What Fulbright Looks For</h2>
      <p>Fulbright is fundamentally an exchange and diplomacy program — not just an academic scholarship. Selection criteria prioritise: academic excellence, project merit and feasibility, ambassadorial potential, community engagement, and the likelihood that the grant will create meaningful cross-cultural exchange. Candidates from underrepresented regions and first-generation applicants are actively encouraged.</p>
      <h2>Application Components</h2>
      <p>The Fulbright application typically requires: personal statement (narrative bio); statement of grant purpose (your specific research/study plan); CV; transcripts; standardised test scores (GRE/TOEFL/IELTS depending on program); and 3 letters of recommendation. Each country's bilateral commission may have additional requirements.</p>
      <h2>Deadlines</h2>
      <p>Deadlines vary dramatically by country — from as early as May to as late as December for programs beginning the following academic year. Check your country's Fulbright Commission website immediately. Do not assume the deadline matches another country's.</p>
      <h2>The Interview</h2>
      <p>Most countries conduct semi-finalist interviews at the national commission level. Interviews focus on your research/study plan, cultural exchange potential, and plans upon return. Demonstrating deep knowledge of both your academic field and your host country's context is essential.</p>
    `,
