    excerpt: "Art and design students have a unique range of scholarships, residencies, and fellowships available. Here are the top funding opportunities for creatives in 2026.",
    content: `<h2>Funding Creative Education</h2><p>Creative fields often feel underfunded compared to STEM, but a growing number of foundations, arts councils, and corporations are investing in the next generation of artists, designers, architects, and filmmakers. The key is knowing where to look.</p>
    <h2>1. Fulbright Arts Grants</h2><p>The Fulbright program includes specific grants for visual artists, performers, and creative professionals to pursue study, teaching, or independent projects in the US or abroad. Both the US Student and US Scholar programs include arts categories.</p>
    <h2>2. Royal College of Art Scholarships (UK)</h2><p>The RCA offers a range of scholarships for exceptional international students across fine art, design, and applied arts. The scholarships are competitive but numerous — the RCA is committed to global accessibility.</p>
    <h2>3. Pratt Institute Merit Scholarships (USA)</h2><p>Pratt Institute in New York offers substantial merit scholarships to exceptional international students in art, design, and architecture. Awards range from $10,000 to full tuition. Portfolio quality is the primary differentiator.</p>
    <h2>4. Chevening Arts Scholarship</h2><p>Several Chevening scholarships are specifically reserved for applicants in the arts and creative industries. These are available in countries where the British Council prioritises creative sector relationships.</p>
    <h2>5. Arts Council Grants (Country-Specific)</h2><p>National arts councils (Arts Council England, Canada Council for the Arts, Australia Council for the Arts, National Endowment for the Arts in the USA) offer grants for individual artists, many of which fund international study or creative residencies.</p>
    <h2>6. Gerrit Rietveld Academie (Netherlands)</h2><p>Amsterdam's prestigious art and design academy offers scholarships for exceptional international students in fine art, design, and critical studies. Applications are competitive and portfolio-based.</p>
    <h2>7. Sundance Institute Fellowships (Film)</h2><p>Sundance offers multiple fellowship programs for emerging filmmakers from underrepresented backgrounds globally. Benefits include mentorship, production funding, and access to the Sundance network.</p>
    <h2>8. Magnum Foundation Photography Grants</h2><p>For documentary photographers and visual artists working on socially engaged projects. The Magnum Foundation funds projects, travel, and editorial development for emerging photographers globally.</p>
    <h2>9. International Architecture Competitions</h2><p>Architecture students frequently access funding through international competition prizes (WAF, Archmarathon, ArchDaily). These competitions simultaneously build professional portfolios and provide prize money for further study.</p>
    <h2>10. British Council Arts Grants</h2><p>The British Council funds international arts exchange, residencies, and study for creatives from its global network of partner countries. Programs vary by country and change annually.</p>`,
