    content: `
      <h2>Overview of the UK Student Visa</h2>
      <p>The UK Student Visa replaced the Tier 4 (General) student visa in October 2020. It allows international students (18 and over) to study at a UK higher education institution that is a licensed Student Sponsor. Students aged 16–17 apply for the Child Student Visa instead.</p>
      <h2>Step 1: Obtain Your Confirmation of Acceptance for Studies (CAS)</h2>
      <p>Before applying for your visa, you need a Confirmation of Acceptance for Studies (CAS) from your UK university. This is a unique reference number your university generates once you've accepted your offer and paid any required deposit. The CAS number is valid for 6 months and is the foundation of your visa application.</p>
      <h2>Step 2: Ensure You Meet the Financial Requirements</h2>
      <p>You must demonstrate funds to cover: your full first-year tuition fees (as stated on your CAS); plus £1,334/month for up to 9 months for living costs if studying in London, or £1,023/month outside London. These funds must be held continuously for 28 consecutive days, ending no more than 31 days before your application date. Bank statements showing this balance are your primary financial evidence.</p>
      <h2>Step 3: Complete the Online Application</h2>
      <p>Apply on the UK Visas and Immigration (UKVI) online portal. You'll need: your CAS reference number; a valid passport; financial evidence; proof of English proficiency (IELTS UKVI, TOEFL iBT, or an accepted alternative); academic qualifications; parental consent if under 18; and your ATAS certificate if studying a controlled subject (certain STEM fields). The application fee is £490 (as of 2025) and you must also pay the Immigration Health Surcharge (IHS) of £776/year upfront.</p>
      <h2>Step 4: Book and Attend Your Biometric Appointment</h2>
      <p>After submitting your online application, book a biometric appointment at a Visa Application Centre (VAC) in your country. You'll provide fingerprints and a photograph. Bring all original supporting documents to this appointment even if you uploaded copies online.</p>
      <h2>Step 5: Await the Decision</h2>
      <p>UK student visa processing times are typically 3 weeks once you attend your biometric appointment, but can vary significantly by country. Apply at least 6–8 weeks before your course start date. You can track your application online.</p>
      <h2>Step 6: Receive Your Biometric Residence Permit (BRP)</h2>
      <p>Once in the UK, collect your Biometric Residence Permit (BRP) from a designated Post Office within 10 days of arrival. Your BRP is your proof of legal residence for the duration of your studies.</p>
      <h2>Extending or Switching Your UK Student Visa</h2>
      <p>You can extend your UK Student Visa from inside the UK if you continue studying. You can switch from a UK Student Visa to a Graduate Route visa upon completion of your degree — allowing 2–3 years of unrestricted work in the UK.</p>
      <h2>Common UK Student Visa Refusal Reasons</h2>
      <p>Insufficient financial evidence; funds not held for the full 28-day consecutive period; unclear source of funds; inadequate English language evidence; discrepancies in the application; missing ATAS certificate for controlled subjects.</p>
    `,
