    excerpt: "Germany's DAAD scholarship opens doors to world-class research and study. This step-by-step guide covers every DAAD program type and how to apply successfully in 2026.",
    content: `
      <h2>What is DAAD?</h2>
      <p>The German Academic Exchange Service (Deutscher Akademischer Austauschdienst) is one of the world's largest funding organisations for international academic exchange. DAAD offers hundreds of scholarship programs for international students and researchers to study and conduct research in Germany, and for German students and researchers to go abroad.</p>
      <h2>Main DAAD Scholarship Programs for International Students</h2>
      <p><strong>Research Grants for Doctoral Candidates and Young Academics:</strong> Up to 24 months of funding for doctoral and postdoctoral research at German universities. Monthly stipend of approximately €1,200–€1,450 plus allowances. <strong>Study Scholarships for Graduates and Postgraduates (EPOS):</strong> Funds one to two year master's study in Germany in development-related fields. Open to students from developing countries. <strong>Bilateral Scholarship Programs:</strong> Country-specific programs negotiated between Germany and partner nations. Check daad.de for your country's bilateral agreements. <strong>Short-Term Research Grants:</strong> 1–6 months of research visits for doctoral students and postdocs. Ideal for collecting field data or using specific German laboratory facilities.</p>
      <h2>Eligibility Requirements</h2>
      <p>Varies by program. General requirements include: a completed undergraduate or graduate degree; a strong academic record; relevant research or professional experience; a clearly formulated study or research plan; endorsement from a German university professor or department; and language proficiency (German B2/C1 for German-medium, IELTS/TOEFL for English-medium programs).</p>
      <h2>Finding a German Host Institution</h2>
      <p>For research grants, you must identify a German host professor before applying. Email potential supervisors with your CV, a brief research proposal, and a specific question related to their work. German academics respect directness and clarity. DAAD's database of German university departments and the My GUIDE supervisor matching tool are valuable resources.</p>
      <h2>Application Documents</h2>
      <p>The DAAD application typically requires: online application form via the DAAD portal; CV (Europass format recommended); motivation letter (1,000–1,500 words); study or research plan (2–3 pages); academic transcripts and degree certificates; language certificates; publication list (where applicable); 2–3 letters of recommendation; letter of acceptance or support from a German institution; and proof of any scholarships or awards received.</p>
      <h2>Application Deadlines</h2>
      <p>DAAD deadlines vary by program. Common deadlines: October 15 for programs beginning in the following academic year (September); March 15 for programs beginning in October/November. Check daad.de for your specific program's current deadline — these change annually.</p>
      <h2>Selection Process</h2>
      <p>Applications are assessed by DAAD's expert committees. Some programs include interviews. DAAD particularly values: academic excellence, clear and feasible project plans, relevance of Germany for your research goals, and future plans to apply your learning — especially for students from developing countries, where return and impact are emphasised.</p>
    `,
