    excerpt: "Europe offers some of the world's best master's scholarships. This guide shows you how to find them, what's required, and how to build a competitive application in 2026.",
    content: `
      <h2>Why Europe Is Exceptional for Master's Scholarships</h2>
      <p>Europe offers a unique combination for master's scholarship seekers: low or no tuition at public universities in Germany, France, Norway, and Austria; generous EU-funded programs like Erasmus Mundus; bilateral government scholarships; and a vast network of institutional awards at world-class universities. Many Europeans and non-Europeans are unaware of the full scope of what's available.</p>
      <h2>Pan-European Programs</h2>
      <p><strong>Erasmus Mundus Joint Masters:</strong> The flagship European scholarship — full funding for joint master's degrees across 2–3 European universities. Approximately 150 programs available. Highly competitive but extraordinarily comprehensive. Applications typically October–January. <strong>Marie Skłodowska-Curie COFUND:</strong> EU co-funded doctoral and postdoctoral fellowships at European universities. Applications through individual institutions. <strong>EURAXESS:</strong> A portal linking international researchers with European fellowship and grant opportunities. Not a scholarship itself, but an invaluable aggregator.</p>
      <h2>Country-by-Country Overview</h2>
      <p><strong>Germany:</strong> DAAD scholarships, university-specific awards, free public university tuition. <strong>France:</strong> Eiffel Excellence Scholarship (Campus France), very low public university tuition (~€243/year for master's). <strong>Netherlands:</strong> Holland Scholarship (€5,000 from Dutch government), university merit scholarships at Leiden, Utrecht, TU Delft. <strong>Sweden:</strong> Swedish Institute Scholarship — covers tuition and a monthly grant of SEK 11,000 for non-EU students. <strong>Ireland:</strong> Government of Ireland Scholarship, Irish Research Council awards. <strong>Belgium:</strong> VLIR-UOS scholarships for students from developing countries. <strong>Spain:</strong> La Caixa Foundation Fellowships — prestigious and fully funded for European and Latin American students.</p>
      <h2>How to Search Efficiently</h2>
      <p>Use ScholarshipPortal.eu — the EU's official database of scholarships for international students. Filter by destination country, field of study, and scholarship type. Funding from country B for study in country A (e.g., French government funding for study in Germany) also exists and is often less competitive.</p>
      <h2>Building a European Scholarship Application</h2>
      <p>European scholarships tend to emphasise: academic excellence (particularly for technical fields); a clear statement of research interest or career goals; European connection or post-study return plan (for non-EU scholarships targeting development impact); language skills; and international experience or perspective. Europass CV format is widely expected.</p>
      <h2>Application Timeline for 2026 Entry</h2>
      <p>Most European scholarships for September 2026 entry have deadlines between October 2025 and February 2026. The Swedish Institute Scholarship typically closes in February. Erasmus Mundus programs: October–February. DAAD: October and March. Eiffel: January. Dutch Holland Scholarship: February.</p>
    `,
